        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Oil Stain Removal Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Driveway oil sits in the pores rather than on the surface, and most failed removals are attempts to clean something that is not where the scrubbing is happening.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing a Fresh Spill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High pressure drives unabsorbed oil deeper into the slab and spreads it wider, turning a small dark spot into a large grey one. Absorb the free oil first with cat litter or a granular absorbent, then wash.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting One Treatment to Clear an Old Stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Poultices and enzymatic cleaners work in stages, drawing out a fraction of the oil per cycle. On a stain that has been soaking in for years, two or three applications are normal. Stopping after one is why people conclude these products do not work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Solvents That Spread the Stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint thinner and similar solvents dissolve the oil and carry it further into the concrete and out across the surface, which frequently leaves a larger, fainter halo. Absorbent poultices pull oil up and out; solvents alone move it around.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over a Stain That Has Not Come Out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer and coatings do not bond to oil-contaminated concrete, so the treated area becomes a bare patch or a fisheye in the finish. If the stain will not lift, that spot needs grinding back to clean concrete before anything goes over it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Removing <strong class="text-white">oil stains from a driveway</strong> depends on how long the oil has been there. Fresh spills come up with an absorbent and a degreaser. Stains that have soaked in for months or years need a poultice left to draw the oil back out, usually across two or three cycles.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Whatever method you use, absorb before you wash, be patient with repeat applications, and never seal or coat over a stain that has not fully lifted — that is the one shortcut that guarantees a visible failure in the finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 text-center bg-blue-50 p-12 rounded-3xl border border-blue-100 shadow-inner">
